Design and implement a CLI-based task management system that supports multiple users, tracks task progress, and generates productivity reports.
The system will read commands from standard input and print results to standard output.

Problem Description
-------------------

You are given a set of commands to manage tasks assigned between users.

A task:

- Has a creator (the user who created/assigned the task).
- Has an assignee (the user responsible for doing the task).
- Has a title (unique per assignee).
- Belongs to a category (e.g., BugFix, Documentation, Feature).
- Has an estimated number of hours.
- Has a status: PENDING or COMPLETED.

Your program must:

1. Add tasks.
2. Mark tasks as completed.
3. Show tasks for a user, filtered by status.
4. Show a productivity report across all users.
5. Show a category-wise summary of hours.

You can assume:

- User names are case-sensitive strings without spaces.
- Task titles are case-sensitive strings without spaces.
- Categories are case-sensitive strings without spaces.
- Hours are positive integers.

Commands
--------

Each command is a single line. Fields are space-separated.

### 1. ADD_TASK

Assign a task from one user to another.

Format:

ADD_TASK <creator> <assignee> <title> <category> <hours>

- creator: User assigning the task.
- assignee: User responsible for completing the task.
- title: Task title (unique per assignee).
- category: Category name (e.g., BugFix, Documentation, Feature).
- hours: Estimated hours (integer > 0).
- New tasks start in PENDING status.

Behavior:

- If a task with the same title already exists for the same assignee, you may ignore the command or overwrite the existing one (document your choice in code comments).
- For simplicity, you may assume input will not intentionally create duplicates.

Example:

ADD_TASK Alice Bob FixLoginIssue BugFix 3
ADD_TASK Bob Charlie WriteDocs Documentation 2
ADD_TASK Alice Bob AddSignupFeature Feature 5
ADD_TASK Charlie Bob UpdateReadme Documentation 1

### 2. COMPLETE_TASK

Mark a task as completed for a specific user.

Format:

COMPLETE_TASK <assignee> <title>

Behavior:

- If the task exists and is PENDING, mark it as COMPLETED.
- If the task does not exist, ignore the command.
- If the task is already COMPLETED, do nothing.

Example:

COMPLETE_TASK Bob FixLoginIssue
COMPLETE_TASK Bob AddSignupFeature

### 3. SHOW_TASKS

Show tasks for a specific user, with a filter on status.

Format:

SHOW_TASKS <user> <status>

- user: The user whose tasks you want to see (as assignee).
- status:
  - PENDING: Show only pending tasks.
  - COMPLETED: Show only completed tasks.
  - ALL: Show both.

Output:

- One line per task that matches the filter.
- Each line should at least include: title, category, hours, and status.
- If the user has no matching tasks, print nothing or a clear message (be consistent).

Example (conceptual):

For the commands:

ADD_TASK Alice Bob FixLoginIssue BugFix 3
ADD_TASK Alice Bob AddSignupFeature Feature 5
COMPLETE_TASK Bob FixLoginIssue
SHOW_TASKS Bob ALL

Possible output:

FixLoginIssue BugFix 3 COMPLETED
AddSignupFeature Feature 5 PENDING

### 4. SHOW_PRODUCTIVITY

Generate a productivity report across all users.

Format:

SHOW_PRODUCTIVITY

For each user who has at least one task (as assignee), compute:

- total_tasks: Total tasks assigned to the user.
- completed_tasks: Tasks with status COMPLETED.
- completion_percentage: floor((completed_tasks * 100) / total_tasks).

Output:

- One line per user:

<user> <total_tasks> <completed_tasks> <completion_percentage>%

Example (conceptual):

Given:

ADD_TASK Alice Bob FixLoginIssue BugFix 3
ADD_TASK Bob Charlie WriteDocs Documentation 2
ADD_TASK Alice Bob AddSignupFeature Feature 5
ADD_TASK Charlie Bob UpdateReadme Documentation 1
COMPLETE_TASK Bob FixLoginIssue
COMPLETE_TASK Bob AddSignupFeature
SHOW_PRODUCTIVITY

Possible output:

Bob 3 2 66%
Charlie 1 0 0%

(Any consistent ordering of users is acceptable.)

### 5. CATEGORY_SUMMARY

Show total hours per category across all users.

Format:

CATEGORY_SUMMARY

For each category, compute:

- total_hours: Sum of hours for tasks in that category.

You may choose whether to count only completed tasks or all tasks (pending + completed). A common choice is to count all tasks; document your choice in comments.

Output:

- One line per category:

<category> <total_hours>

Example (conceptual):

Using the same commands:

CATEGORY_SUMMARY

Possible output:

BugFix 3
Documentation 3
Feature 5

Sample End-to-End Input
-----------------------

ADD_TASK Alice Bob FixLoginIssue BugFix 3
ADD_TASK Bob Charlie WriteDocs Documentation 2
ADD_TASK Alice Bob AddSignupFeature Feature 5
ADD_TASK Charlie Bob UpdateReadme Documentation 1
COMPLETE_TASK Bob FixLoginIssue
COMPLETE_TASK Bob AddSignupFeature
SHOW_TASKS Bob ALL
SHOW_PRODUCTIVITY
CATEGORY_SUMMARY

You should define and document your exact output format clearly and keep it consistent, even if it differs in minor formatting details from the examples above.

Expectations and Guidelines
---------------------------

- Use a modular, object-oriented design where appropriate (for example, classes such as Task and TaskManager).
- Keep responsibilities separated; avoid putting all logic into a single class or method.
- Design the system so that it is easy to extend (e.g., to add new commands or new report types).
- Handle edge cases gracefully (e.g., completing non-existent tasks, listing tasks for a user with no tasks).
- The program should read commands until EOF (or until some explicit exit command if you add one) and be demo-able via a main driver program or tests.
- You may use any programming language and standard libraries you prefer.

Other Notes:
Do not use any database or NoSQL store, use in-memory data-structure for now.
Do not create any UI for the application.
Write a driver class for demo purpose. Which will execute all the commands at one place in the code and have test cases.
Please prioritize code compilation, execution and completion.
Work on the expected output first and then add good-to-have features of your own.